    "lawCode": "Amd §22-c, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5475\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to five-year capital\nplans for the state university of New York and the city university of\nNew York\n\n \nPURPOSE::\n\nTo provide five-year capital plans for SUNY and CUNY for the fiscal year\nbeginning on April 1, 2021.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1. Amends section 22-c of the state finance law to direct the\nGovernor to submit a five-year capital plan that meets 100 percent of\nthe critical maintenance needs identified by each public university\nsystem or annually reduces the facility condition index for each univer-\nsity system.\n\nSection 2. Provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs part of the fiscal year 2008-2009 budget, the Legislature enacted a\nfive-year capital plan with annual critical maintenance appropriations\nof $550 million for the State University of New York and $284.2 million\nfor the City University of New York. These appropriations provided the\nState's public colleges and universities with the ability to plan for\ncapital expenditures and substantially reduce the backlog of critical\nmaintenance projects. Since the conclusion of that plan in the 2012-2013\nbudget cycle, the budget process has failed to produce a similarly\nrobust capital plan for the SUNY and CUNY systems. As such, the academic\nfacilities at our public colleges and universities, as well as the three\nSUNY hospitals, are in danger of not receiving the annual investment\nnecessary for proper maintenance.\n\nThis bill would incorporate capital planning for SUNY and CUNY into the\noverall capital plan submitted to the Legislature by requiring that the\nGovernor submit five-year capital plans for SUNY and CUNY as part of the\nExecutive Budget. This requirement would ensure that the facilities at\nour public institutions of higher education receive the capital funding\nnecessary to meet their annual critical maintenance needs or reduce the\nfacility condition index which measures the current condition of facili-\nties requiring capital investment.\n\nAlthough this bill was vetoed by the Governor in 2015, 2017, and 2018,\nthis measure is still of the utmost importance. Ensuring, capital fund-\ning for SUNY and CUNY is essential to their ability to continue to\nprovide exemplary education for students of the State of New York.\nBuilding infrastructure across SUNY and CUNY campuses remains in dire\nneed of critical maintenance because buildings are crumbling under the\nstudents' feet. This funding will further assist the efforts to reduce\nefforts to reduce facility condition indexes.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\n2020:S.3548A- Passed senate.\n\n2019:S.3548- Passed Senate.\n\n2018:S.7236(Veto319);\n\n2017:S.1625(Veto205);\n\n2015:S.5217/A.7388\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nImmediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
